keep blue light off the slow paths

The shelf, the menus and the cart animations were still at 30 fps on 0048,
and on either cpu: the draw ran on cpu 1 for 540 of the shelf's 600 frames
and missed 369 of them. The card had blue light on at step 8, where it was
off this morning, and blue light is what changed the costs:

- the shelf goes out through the graded copy, which wrote the panel a byte
  at a time: 9.4 ms a frame against 2.7 for the straight copy. The panel is
  uncached memory, so each byte was its own write;
- everything drawn over a game is tinted by the gain, and a tinted sprite
  multiplied every texel by it in floats: the in-game menu's labels and its
  footer at 92-119 ns a pixel against 20-28 untinted, 7.9 ms of sprites in a
  menu frame.

The graded copy now grades a row into RAM and copies the row to the panel
whole, and so do the two mirrored straight copies, the shelf's and the
overlay's, which reversed four bytes at a time into the panel. A texture
drawn tinted keeps a copy of itself with the tint's colour already applied,
built once per texture and tint -- once a blue light step, in practice --
and the blit takes each texel's colour from it and its alpha from the
per-draw table; `modulate` treats the channels apart, so the bytes are the
same.

The device costs above are from the 0048 trace. On the host neither change
shows -- its panel in the tests is RAM and its floats are cheap -- so what
they buy on the Flip is not yet measured.

Tests: a pre-tinted blit matches tinting every texel at 1:1, scaled up and
down, clipped, faded and not, under three tints; the graded copy out is
the canvas through the gain table, mirrored and not. Swapping two channels
in the pre-tint fails three tests; leaving the copy ungraded fails two.

Applies on top of 0048, upstream 91499a9.

